  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "PcURp8CLtOeJzSd1FpgM3IezRmM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:15.023Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Avaloq appears in the Flowable Partner Finder, but the entry describes an OEM relationship: the engine is embedded in Avaloq's own core banking software. The research draws the conclusion directly \u2014 vendor, not an integrator \u2014 and records 2,000+ staff.\n\nThe distinction decides what kind of conversation is possible. In this corpus an integrator is a firm that could place a Powerhouse component inside a client project it is already delivering; an OEM would instead have to adopt that component into its own product roadmap, on its own release cycle, against its own build-versus-buy logic. Directory membership looks identical in both cases, which is exactly why the flag records the difference explicitly.\n\nIt is also worth noting how thin the underlying provenance is. The row was mined from the vendor's partner page on 2026-08-18 and was not imprint-swept, so no principal or contact address was resolved. Two further OEM entries in the same sweep \u2014 Exentra and eligamo \u2014 carry the same shape, which means the Partner Finder cannot be read as a channel list without first separating the product vendors from the delivery firms.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**unprioritised**\n- Avaloq (CH Zurich) \u2014 avaloq.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> OEM PARTNER \u2014 core banking software, 2,000+ staff. Vendor, not an integrator \u00b7 SOURCE: Flowable Partner Finder (flowable.com/company/partners), mined 2026-08-18. NOT imprint-swept.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:29:15.023399Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:15.023404Z"
    }
  },
